# Historical basis: Fakhreddin Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of Democracy,
# 1941–1953, pp. 201–208; Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions,
# ch. 5, pp. 249–253. The correspondence network and effects are
# counterfactual abstractions.
title: Political Correspondence
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: party_affairs
view-if: political_correspondence_timer <= 0 and front_formed = 0
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; political_correspondence_timer = 3

= Political Correspondence

Editors, lawyers, party branches, prospective candidates, and personal
correspondents already know the political conditions in which they work. What
the opposition lacks is a common way to circulate their arguments, coordinate
requests, and preserve records across organizations that answer to different
leaders.

Concentrating the correspondence can make one channel more regular. The choice
is which existing relationships should carry the opposition's common work.

- @correspondence_editors
- @correspondence_provinces
- @correspondence_party
- @return_card

@correspondence_editors
title: Link the friendly editors
subtitle: Papers compare what they can print and verify while preserving their own editorial lines
on-arrival: {!
Q.political_correspondence_repeat =
    Q.political_correspondence_last === "editors" ? 1 : 0;
Q.political_correspondence_last = "editors";
Q.press_capacity += Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.press_capacity < 70 ? 3 : 1);
Q.constitutional_legitimacy += Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 0 :
    (Q.constitutional_legitimacy < 70 ? 1 : 0);
!}

Editors exchange summaries of restrictions, constitutional arguments, and
reports that require corroboration. They coordinate enough to avoid
contradicting one another on basic facts, but no paper becomes the organ of a
coalition that has not formed.

The opposition gains a more coherent press conversation without acquiring
control over it.
[? if political_correspondence_repeat = 1:
The same editors resume their exchange. This time the work is maintenance:
checking a fresh restriction, correcting a doubtful report, and keeping
separate editorial lines from drifting into contradiction. The channel
survives, but repetition does not create another newspaper or a common
editorial command. ?]
[? if press_capacity >= 75: The editors already know how to reach one another.
Another exchange keeps their accounts aligned, but does not create another
paper or a common editorial command. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@correspondence_provinces
title: Recruit provincial correspondents
subtitle: Regular letters connect provincial candidates and lawyers to Tehran without placing them under a central command
on-arrival: {!
Q.political_correspondence_repeat =
    Q.political_correspondence_last === "provinces" ? 1 : 0;
Q.political_correspondence_last = "provinces";
Q.organizational_reach += Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.organizational_reach < 70 ? 3 : 1);
Q.independent_nationalists_organization +=
    Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.independent_nationalists_organization < 70 ? 2 : 1);
!}

Prospective candidates, lawyers, and editors ask trusted acquaintances outside Tehran to
send dated reports, requests for assistance, and copies of material needed in
Tehran. The correspondence gives people who already understand their own
constituencies a dependable route into common legal and political work.

Independent politicians gain connections that do not depend entirely on a
single established party.
[? if political_correspondence_repeat = 1:
Letters go back to the correspondents already recruited. They clarify missing
dates, carry replies from Tehran, and keep existing routes alive; they do not
magically supply trusted contacts in provinces where none was found. ?]
[? if organizational_reach >= 75: The dependable correspondents are already
writing. Further requests make the exchange more regular, but add few places
where no trusted relationship existed. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@correspondence_party
title: Strengthen the <span class="term term-constitutionalist">Iran Party</span> circuit
subtitle: A disciplined party channel moves reports reliably, while other circles remain outside it
on-arrival: {!
Q.political_correspondence_repeat =
    Q.political_correspondence_last === "party" ? 1 : 0;
Q.political_correspondence_last = "party";
Q.iran_party_organization += Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.iran_party_organization < 70 ? 3 : 1);
Q.iran_party_relation += Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.iran_party_relation < 70 ? 2 : 1);
Q.organizational_reach += Q.political_correspondence_repeat ? 0 :
    (Q.organizational_reach < 65 ? 1 : 0);
!}

{!<span class="term term-constitutionalist">!}Iran Party{!</span>!}
organizers regularize reports between local contacts and Tehran. The circuit
is narrower than a general opposition network but more dependable within the
organization that maintains it.

The party becomes a more useful partner. Its correspondence is not a neutral
register of every opposition group.
[? if political_correspondence_repeat = 1:
Party organizers work the same circuit again, chasing unanswered letters and
standardizing reports from contacts already inside it. Reliability improves at
the margin, while politicians outside the party remain outside its command. ?]
[? if iran_party_organization >= 75: The party circuit is now dependable.
Additional discipline mostly strengthens routes it already controls, which
does not answer the other opposition circles' need for independent contact. ?]

- @post_event: Continue
